## 1. Introduction

The task-based approach to automation models the economy at the level of tasks rather than jobs. A job is a bundle of tasks; AI alters the cost, productivity, and feasibility of the tasks that compose it. This architecture separates three distinct effects: automation (displacement of labor from tasks), augmentation (productivity gains for labor in tasks), and reinstatement (creation of new labor-intensive tasks). The central theoretical question is whether these forces, operating through the labor market, generate bounded reallocation or structural technological unemployment.

This paper provides a corrected formulation of the task-based macroeconomic architecture of transformative AI. It resolves three technical inconsistencies that affect the main propositions. First, the aggregate labor share is derived using the across-task elasticity of substitution \(\varepsilon\), not the within-task elasticity \(\sigma\). Under binary automation, the within-task elasticity drops out of the labor share entirely, and the across-task elasticity governs how expenditure is allocated between automated and non-automated tasks. Second, the task-level labor demand equation is corrected to include the unit-cost factor \(c_t(i)^\sigma/A_{L,t}\) that is omitted in earlier formulations; this omission propagates into the reallocation equation and the bounded-reallocation theorem. Third, the welfare wedge in the constrained-inefficiency result is reformulated with a genuine marginal-utility ratio between workers and capitalists, rather than a tautological ratio of identical terms.

The analysis proceeds as follows. Section 2 establishes the formal environment. Section 3 derives task-level production and cost functions. Section 4 aggregates to the economy-wide labor share and proves the corrected dual-capital labor share proposition. Section 5 models the innovation margin between automation and reinstatement R&D. Section 6 derives labor demand and the reallocation equation from primitives. Section 7 proves the bounded-reallocation theorem under explicit assumptions. Section 8 characterizes the breakdown of the safety valve when both cognitive and physical automation are active. Section 9 analyzes demand-side feedback and proves constrained inefficiency of the decentralized equilibrium. Section 10 models capital ownership and fiscal capacity. Section 11 describes the computational strategy and provides a baseline calibration. Section 12 concludes.

