## 7. Embodied AI and the Breakdown of the Safety Valve

### 7.1 Dual-Capital Production with Physical Automation

When humanoid robotics and autonomous physical infrastructure reach economic viability, the physical sector ceases to function as a safe harbor for displaced cognitive workers. The task-level production function for physical tasks is given by (5), with \(\alpha_{P,i} = \Phi(\rho_i, \tau_{\text{robotics}})\).

### 7.2 Multidimensional Net Displacement

The net displacement vector becomes:
\[
\mathbf{D}_t^{\text{net}} = \begin{bmatrix} \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - n_{C,t} \\ \mathcal{A}_{P,t} - n_{P,t} \end{bmatrix}. \tag{52}
\]

Labor market clearing requires:
\[
L_t = L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}. \tag{53}
\]

When both cognitive and physical automation expand simultaneously \((\mathcal{A}_{C,t} > 0\) and \(\mathcal{A}_{P,t} > 0)\), the traditional non-cognitive safety valve disappears if physical tasks are also automated.

### 7.3 Proposition 3 (Breakdown of the Safety Valve)

**Proposition 3.** Assume:

1. Labor supply is fixed: \(L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}\).
2. Cognitive automation is active: \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} > 0\).
3. Physical automation is active: \(\mathcal{A}_{P,t} > 0\).
4. Displaced cognitive and physical workers are absorbed only by the interpersonal sector \(I\).
5. The interpersonal sector has bounded absorption capacity \(\kappa_I \bar{L}\), where:
\[
\kappa_I = \kappa_0 \left(\frac{w_{I,t}}{w_t}\right)^{\epsilon_I}, \qquad \kappa_0 \in (0,1), \ \epsilon_I > 0. \tag{54}
\]

Define displaced labor as:
\[
D_{C,t} = -\Delta L_{C,t} > 0, \qquad D_{P,t} = -\Delta L_{P,t} > 0. \tag{55}
\]

Then:

**(i)** If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} \leq \kappa_I \bar{L}\), displaced labor is fully absorbed by the interpersonal sector and no structural unemployment emerges.

**(ii)** If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} > \kappa_I \bar{L}\), structural technological unemployment emerges:
\[
U_t^* = D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} - \kappa_I \bar{L} > 0. \tag{56}
\]

**(iii)** The aggregate labor share converges to the share of newly created labor tasks:
\[
s_{L,t} \to s_{L,n} = \frac{w_t L_{n,t}}{Y_t} \quad \text{as} \quad \mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1, \tag{57}
\]
where \(L_{n,t}\) is employment in newly created tasks. This limit is strictly positive as long as \(L_{n,t} > 0\).

**Proof.** Parts (i) and (ii) follow directly from labor market clearing and the definition of \(\kappa_I \bar{L}\). For part (iii), from Proposition 1:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}. \tag{58}
\]
As \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1\), we have \(N_t \to n_t + (1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t})\). The mass of non-automated tasks goes to zero. The only tasks remaining are newly created tasks of mass \(n_t\). Since newly created tasks are labor-intensive by construction:
\[
s_{L,t} \to \frac{w_t L_{n,t}}{Y_t} > 0, \tag{59}
\]
as long as \(L_{n,t} > 0\). \(\square\)

**Remark 1.** The correct limit is the share of newly created labor tasks, not the sum of interpersonal and new-task shares. When \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1\), the mass of interpersonal tasks also goes to zero because interpersonal tasks are non-automated and lie in the same task continuum. The only remaining labor tasks are the newly created ones.

**Remark 2.** Embodied AI removes the physical fallback, but the reinstatement fallback may remain. The question is whether new tasks have enough absorption capacity and demand.

### 7.4 Macroeconomic Implications

The aggregate labor share faces downward pressure from both cognitive software and physical robotics:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}} \to s_{L,n} \quad \text{as} \quad (\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t}) \to 1. \tag{60}
\]

Capital returns diverge:
\[
\max \left( \frac{\dot{r}_{C,t}}{r_{C,t}}, \frac{\dot{r}_{P,t}}{r_{P,t}} \right) \gg \frac{\dot{w}_t}{w_t}. \tag{61}
\]

Transitional unemployment becomes more severe because physical and cognitive skills both undergo structural obsolescence.

