## Appendix B: Derivation of the Aggregate Labor Share (Proposition 1)

### B.1 The CES Aggregator

Aggregate output is:
\[
Y_t = \left[ \frac{1}{M_t} \int_0^{M_t} y_t(i)^{\frac{\varepsilon-1}{\varepsilon}} \, di \right]^{\frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1}}, \qquad \varepsilon > 0, \tag{B.1}
\]
where \(M_t = 1 + n_t\) is the total task mass. The normalization by \(M_t\) ensures that the aggregate is well-defined when the task mass expands through reinstatement.

The aggregate price index \(P_t\) is defined implicitly by \(P_t Y_t = \int_0^{M_t} p_t(i) y_t(i) \, di\), where \(p_t(i)\) is the price of task \(i\). Under monopolistic competition with flexible prices, \(p_t(i) = \frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1} c_t(i)\). The price index satisfies:
\[
P_t = \left[ \frac{1}{M_t} \int_0^{M_t} p_t(i)^{1-\varepsilon} \, di \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.2}
\]

Under (A1)–(A3), the economy partitions into three sets:

- Non-automated tasks: mass \(N_t\), unit cost \(c_{\text{non-auto},t} = w_t/A_{L,t}\).
- Automated cognitive tasks: mass \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t}\), unit cost \(c_{C,\text{auto},t} = r_{C,t}/A_{C,t}\).
- Automated physical tasks: mass \(\mathcal{A}_{P,t}\), unit cost \(c_{P,\text{auto},t} = r_{P,t}/A_{P,t}\).

The price index becomes:
\[
P_t = \left[ \frac{N_t}{M_t} c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}{M_t} c_{C,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}}{M_t} c_{P,\text{auto},t}^{1-\varepsilon} \right]^{\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.3}
\]

### B.2 Aggregate Labor Demand

The demand for task \(i\) is:
\[
y_t(i) = \left(\frac{p_t(i)}{P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.4}
\]

For a non-automated task, labor input is:
\[
l_t(i) = \frac{1}{A_{L,t}} y_t(i) = \frac{1}{A_{L,t}} \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1} \frac{w_t}{A_{L,t} P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.5}
\]

Aggregating over the mass \(N_t\) of non-automated tasks:
\[
L_t = N_t \frac{1}{A_{L,t}} \left(\frac{\varepsilon}{\varepsilon-1} \frac{w_t}{A_{L,t} P_t}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{Y_t}{P_t}. \tag{B.6}
\]

### B.3 Aggregate Labor Share

The aggregate labor share is:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t L_t}{P_t Y_t}. \tag{B.7}
\]

Using Shephard's lemma on the price index (B.3):
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t}{P_t} \frac{\partial P_t}{\partial w_t}. \tag{B.8}
\]

Computing the derivative:
\[
\frac{\partial P_t}{\partial w_t} = \frac{1}{1-\varepsilon} P_t^{\varepsilon} \cdot \frac{N_t}{M_t} (1-\varepsilon) c_{\text{non-auto},t}^{-\varepsilon} \frac{\partial c_{\text{non-auto},t}}{\partial w_t}. \tag{B.9}
\]

Since \(c_{\text{non-auto},t} = w_t/A_{L,t}\), we have \(\partial c_{\text{non-auto},t}/\partial w_t = 1/A_{L,t}\). Substituting:
\[
\frac{\partial P_t}{\partial w_t} = P_t^{\varepsilon} \frac{N_t}{M_t} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{1}{A_{L,t}}. \tag{B.10}
\]

Therefore:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{w_t}{P_t} \cdot P_t^{\varepsilon} \frac{N_t}{M_t} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{-\varepsilon} \frac{1}{A_{L,t}} = \frac{N_t}{M_t} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} P_t^{\varepsilon-1}. \tag{B.11}
\]

From the price index (B.3):
\[
P_t^{1-\varepsilon} = \frac{N_t}{M_t} \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}}{M_t} \left(\frac{r_{C,t}}{A_{C,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \frac{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}}{M_t} \left(\frac{r_{P,t}}{A_{P,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}. \tag{B.12}
\]

Hence:
\[
P_t^{\varepsilon-1} = \frac{M_t}{N_t \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} \left(\frac{r_{C,t}}{A_{C,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \left(\frac{r_{P,t}}{A_{P,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.13}
\]

Substituting into (B.11):
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}}{N_t \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} \left(\frac{r_{C,t}}{A_{C,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \left(\frac{r_{P,t}}{A_{P,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}}. \tag{B.14}
\]

This is the corrected labor share. The exponent is \(1-\varepsilon\), not \(1-\sigma\).

**Proposition 1 (Closed-Form Labor Share with Dual Capital).** Under (A1)–(A3), symmetric task aggregation, and binary automation, the aggregate labor share of income is:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}, \tag{B.15}
\]
where
\[
N_t = 1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t} + n_t, \qquad \mathcal{A}_{j,t} = m_{j,t} d_{j,t}, \tag{B.16}
\]
\[
X_t = \left(\frac{w_t}{A_{L,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}, \qquad Z_{j,t} = \left(\frac{r_{j,t}}{A_{j,t}}\right)^{1-\varepsilon}, \qquad j \in \{C, P\}. \tag{B.17}
\]

**Proof.** Let \(\mathcal{N}_t = N_t X_t\) and \(\mathcal{A}_t = \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}\). Under (A1)–(A3), the aggregate labor share equals the ratio of labor-weighted to total factor-weighted task masses. The labor-weighted task mass is \(\mathcal{N}_t\), and the capital-weighted task mass is \(\mathcal{A}_t\). Hence:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{\mathcal{N}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t}. \tag{B.18}
\]
Substituting the definitions yields (B.15). \(\square\)

**Remark 1.** When \(\mathcal{A}_{P,t} = 0\), the formula collapses to the single-capital expression. When \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} = \mathcal{A}_{P,t} = 0\), \(s_{L,t} = 1\).

**Remark 2.** The across-task elasticity \(\varepsilon\) governs the labor share, not the within-task elasticity \(\sigma\). Under binary automation, the within-task elasticity drops out because non-automated tasks have labor cost share one and automated tasks have labor cost share zero. The elasticity that determines how expenditure is allocated between these two types is \(\varepsilon\), the elasticity of substitution across tasks.

### B.4 Log-Linearization

Take logs of (B.15):
\[
\ln s_{L,t} = \ln \mathcal{N}_t - \ln(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t). \tag{B.19}
\]

Differentiate:
\[
\hat{s}_{L,t} = \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \frac{\mathcal{N}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t} \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \frac{\mathcal{A}_t}{\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_t}. \tag{B.20}
\]

Since \(\mathcal{N}_t/(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t) = s_{L,t}\) and \(\mathcal{A}_t/(\mathcal{N}_t + \mathcal{A}_t) = 1 - s_{L,t}\):
\[
\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} - \widehat{\mathcal{A}_t} \right]. \tag{B.21}
\]

Now:
\[
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_t} = \widehat{N_t} + \hat{X}_t = \widehat{N_t} + (1-\varepsilon)(\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}), \tag{B.22}
\]
\[
\widehat{\mathcal{A}_t} = \omega_{C,t} \left(\widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} + \hat{Z}_{C,t}\right) + \omega_{P,t} \left(\widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + \hat{Z}_{P,t}\right), \tag{B.23}
\]
where
\[
\omega_{j,t} = \frac{\mathcal{A}_{j,t} Z_{j,t}}{\mathcal{A}_t}, \qquad \omega_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} = 1. \tag{B.24}
\]

Substituting:
\[
\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\varepsilon)(\hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t}) - \omega_{C,t} \hat{Z}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{Z}_{P,t} \right]. \tag{B.25}
\]

Using \(\hat{Z}_{j,t} = (1-\varepsilon)(\hat{r}_{j,t} - \hat{A}_{j,t})\):
\[
\hat{s}_{L,t} = (1 - s_{L,t}) \left[ \widehat{N_t} - \omega_{C,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{C,t}} - \omega_{P,t} \widehat{\mathcal{A}_{P,t}} + (1-\varepsilon) \left( \hat{w}_t - \hat{A}_{L,t} - \omega_{C,t} \hat{r}_{C,t} - \omega_{P,t} \hat{r}_{P,t} + \omega_{C,t} \hat{A}_{C,t} + \omega_{P,t} \hat{A}_{P,t} \right) \right]. \tag{B.26}
\]

This confirms equation (26) in the main text.

