## Appendix E: Proof of Proposition 3 (Breakdown of the Safety Valve)

### E.1 Statement

**Proposition 3.** Under Assumptions 1–5:

1. Fixed labor supply: \(L_{C,t} + L_{P,t} + L_{I,t} = \bar{L}\).
2. Cognitive automation is active: \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} > 0\).
3. Physical automation is active: \(\mathcal{A}_{P,t} > 0\).
4. Displaced workers are absorbed only by the interpersonal sector \(I\).
5. The interpersonal sector has bounded absorption capacity \(\kappa_I \bar{L}\), with \(\kappa_I = \kappa_0 (w_{I,t}/w_t)^{\epsilon_I}\).

Then:

(i) If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} \leq \kappa_I \bar{L}\), no structural unemployment.

(ii) If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} > \kappa_I \bar{L}\), structural unemployment \(U_t^* = D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} - \kappa_I \bar{L} > 0\).

(iii) \(s_{L,t} \to s_{L,n}\) as \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1\).

### E.2 Proof of Parts (i) and (ii)

Labor market clearing:
\[
L_{I,t} = \bar{L} - L_{C,t} - L_{P,t}. \tag{E.1}
\]

Displaced workers:
\[
D_{C,t} = -\Delta L_{C,t}, \qquad D_{P,t} = -\Delta L_{P,t}. \tag{E.2}
\]

The interpersonal sector can absorb up to \(\kappa_I \bar{L}\) workers.

If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} \leq \kappa_I \bar{L}\), all displaced workers are absorbed, so \(U_t^* = 0\).

If \(D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} > \kappa_I \bar{L}\), the residual:
\[
U_t^* = D_{C,t} + D_{P,t} - \kappa_I \bar{L} > 0 \tag{E.3}
\]
remains unemployed. This is structural technological unemployment. \(\square\)

### E.3 Proof of Part (iii)

From Proposition 1:
\[
s_{L,t} = \frac{N_t X_t}{N_t X_t + \mathcal{A}_{C,t} Z_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} Z_{P,t}}. \tag{E.4}
\]

As \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1\):
\[
N_t = 1 - \mathcal{A}_{C,t} - \mathcal{A}_{P,t} + n_t \to n_t. \tag{E.5}
\]

The mass of non-automated tasks goes to \(n_t\). Since interpersonal tasks are part of the non-automated continuum, their mass also goes to zero unless they are newly created. The only remaining labor tasks are the newly created ones of mass \(n_t\). Since newly created tasks are labor-intensive by construction:
\[
s_{L,t} \to \frac{w_t L_{n,t}}{Y_t} = s_{L,n} > 0, \tag{E.6}
\]
as long as \(L_{n,t} > 0\). \(\square\)

**Remark.** The correct limit is the share of newly created labor tasks, not the sum of interpersonal and new-task shares. When \(\mathcal{A}_{C,t} + \mathcal{A}_{P,t} \to 1\), the mass of interpersonal tasks also goes to zero because interpersonal tasks are non-automated and lie in the same task continuum.

### E.4 Absorption Capacity

The absorption capacity \(\kappa_I\) is:
\[
\kappa_I = \kappa_0 \left(\frac{w_{I,t}}{w_t}\right)^{\epsilon_I}, \qquad \kappa_0 \in (0,1), \ \epsilon_I > 0. \tag{E.7}
\]

This specification allows absorption capacity to increase when interpersonal wages rise relative to the average wage. The parameter \(\epsilon_I\) is the demand elasticity for interpersonal services.

**Microfoundation.** Suppose the demand for interpersonal services is:
\[
Y_{I,t} = \kappa_0 \left(\frac{P_{I,t}}{P_t}\right)^{-\epsilon_I} Y_t. \tag{E.8}
\]

With linear production \(Y_{I,t} = A_{I,t} L_{I,t}\) and competitive pricing \(P_{I,t} = w_{I,t}/A_{I,t}\), the absorption capacity in employment terms is:
\[
\kappa_I \bar{L} = \frac{Y_{I,t}}{A_{I,t}} = \kappa_0 \left(\frac{w_{I,t}}{w_t}\right)^{-\epsilon_I} \frac{Y_t}{A_{I,t}} \cdot \frac{w_t}{P_t}. \tag{E.9}
\]

Under balanced growth with \(A_{I,t}\) and \(P_t\) growing at the same rate, this reduces to (E.7).

