              |  LABOR SHARE        |
              |  s_L,t = ...        |
              +---------------------+

STICKY WAGES:
  w_t = w_{t-1}(1 + pi_t^w)
  pi_t^w = kappa_w (Y_hat_t - Y_hat*)
  kappa_w in (0,1)

DEMAND EXTERNALITY:
  dY_t/ds_L,t > 0    (higher labor share => higher demand)
  ds_L,t/dA_j,t < 0  (more automation => lower labor share)

  => dY_t/dA_j,t = (dY_t/ds_L,t)(ds_L,t/dA_j,t) < 0

  FIRMS DO NOT INTERNALIZE:
    Automation reduces the high-MPC wage bill
    => reduces aggregate demand
    => creates a wedge between private and social returns

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                                                 |
|   PROPOSITION 4 (CORRECTED):                                                    |
|                                                                                 |
|   The social marginal cost of automation exceeds the private marginal cost by: |
|                                                                                 |
|     W_t = - (dY_t/ds_L,t) * (ds_L,t/dA_j,t) * (lambda_W,t - lambda_K,t)/lambda_t|
|                                                                                 |
|   where:                                                                        |
|     lambda_W,t = chi_W u'(C_W,t)   (marginal utility of worker consumption)     |
|     lambda_K,t = chi_K u'(C_K,t)   (marginal utility of capitalist consumption) |
|     lambda_t   = shadow value of resource constraint                            |
|                                                                                 |
|   This is a genuine distributional wedge, not a tautological ratio.             |
|                                                                                 |
|   DERIVATIVE OF LABOR SHARE:                                                    |
|                                                                                 |
|     ds_L,t/dA_j,t = - [X_t A_t + N_t X_t Z_j,t] / v_t^2                         |
|                                                                                 |
|   where A_t = A_C,t Z_C,t + A_P,t Z_P,t                                         |
|         v_t = N_t X_t + A_C,t Z_C,t + A_P,t Z_P,t                               |
|                                                                                 |
|   The term X_t A_t captures the effect of automation on N_t.                    |
|   Omitting it understates the externality.                                      |
|                                                                                 |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

