## Diagram 10: Timeline of Automation and Reinstatement (Corrected)

```
Time t ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------>

PHASE 1: COGNITIVE AUTOMATION
  - m_C,t d_C,t rises
  - A_C,t rises
  - L_C,t falls (labor demand elasticity -epsilon)
  - L_I,t rises (reallocation)
  - n_t rises (reinstatement R&D responds to w_t/r_t)
  - s_L,t falls but bounded
  - epsilon < 1: labor share relatively stable
  - epsilon > 1: labor share falls rapidly

  KEY EQUATION:
    Delta L_I,t = L_C,t-1 [ varsigma_C,t A_hat_C,t
                             + (epsilon-1)(Y_hat_t - A_hat_L,t) ]

PHASE 2: EMBODIED AUTOMATION
  - m_P,t d_P,t rises
  - A_P,t rises
  - L_P,t falls
  - L_I,t absorbs displaced workers
  - Absorption capacity kappa_I L_bar may bind
  - If D_C,t + D_P,t > kappa_I L_bar:
      U_t* = D_C,t + D_P,t - kappa_I L_bar > 0
  - Structural technological unemployment emerges

  KEY EQUATION:
    U_t* = max(0, D_C,t + D_P,t - kappa_I L_bar)

PHASE 3: FULL AUTOMATION (LIMIT)
  - A_C,t + A_P,t -> 1
  - N_t -> n_t
  - The interpersonal sector I is also part of the non-automated continuum
  - Its mass also -> 0
  - Only newly created tasks n_t remain
  - s_L,t -> s_L,n = w_t L_n,t / Y_t
  - If n_t = 0 and all tasks automated: s_L,t -> 0
  - Fiscal capacity depends on tau_K, theta_W
  - Ownership dynamics: theta_{W,t+1} = theta_{W,t} + zeta s_L,t Y_t

  KEY LIMIT:
    s_L,t -> s_L,n   (NOT s_L,I + s_L,n)
    because I is part of the same continuum and its mass -> 0

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              SUMMARY OF CORRECTIONS                                          |
|                                                                                             |
|  1. LABOR SHARE EXPONENT:                                                                    |
|     Earlier: X_t = (w_t/A_L,t)^(1-sigma)                                                     |
|     Corrected: X_t = (w_t/A_L,t)^(1-epsilon)                                                 |
|     Reason: Under binary automation, sigma drops out; epsilon governs across-task            |
|     allocation.                                                                              |
|                                                                                             |
|  2. LABOR DEMAND:                                                                            |
|     Earlier: l_t(i) = (w_t/A_L,t)^(-sigma)(1-alpha)^sigma(c_t/P_t)^(-epsilon)Y_t/P_t        |
|     Corrected: l_t(i) = (1/A_L,t)(eps/(eps-1) w_t/(A_L,t P_t))^(-epsilon)Y_t/P_t            |
|     Reason: The omitted factor c_t(i)^sigma/A_L,t makes sigma drop out.                      |
|                                                                                             |
|  3. REALLOCATION EQUATION:                                                                   |
|     Earlier: Delta L_I,t = L_C,t-1 [ varsigma_C,t A_hat_C,t - sigma A_hat_L,t               |
|                                       + (sigma-1) Y_hat_t ]                                  |
|     Corrected: Delta L_I,t = L_C,t-1 [ varsigma_C,t A_hat_C,t                               |
|                                         + (epsilon-1)(Y_hat_t - A_hat_L,t) ]                 |
|     Reason: Correct labor demand implies epsilon, not sigma, governs reallocation.           |
|                                                                                             |
|  4. INNOVATION SHARE:                                                                        |
|     Earlier: z_t* = 1 / [ 1 + (pi_A/pi_N)(r_t/w_t)^(2 eta) ]                                |
|     Corrected: z_t* = [ (pi_N/pi_A)(w_t/r_t)^(2 eta) ]^(1/(1-phi))                          |
|                        / [ 1 + (same) ]                                                      |
|     Reason: The earlier formula was the automation share, not the reinstatement share.       |
|     Also added phi for diminishing returns.                                                  |
|                                                                                             |
|  5. WELFARE WEDGE:                                                                           |
|     Earlier: W_t = (dY_t/ds_L,t)(ds_L,t/dA_j,t)(dU/dC_t)/(dU/dC_t) > 0                      |
|     Corrected: W_t = -(dY_t/ds_L,t)(ds_L,t/dA_j,t)(lambda_W,t - lambda_K,t)/lambda_t > 0     |
|     Reason: The ratio of identical terms was tautological. The corrected wedge uses          |
|     a genuine marginal-utility difference between workers and capitalists.                   |
|                                                                                             |
|  6. LABOR SHARE LIMIT:                                                                       |
|     Earlier: s_L,t -> s_L,I + s_L,n                                                          |
|     Corrected: s_L,t -> s_L,n                                                                |
|     Reason: When A_C + A_P -> 1, the interpersonal sector I is also part of the              |
|     non-automated continuum and its mass -> 0. Only newly created tasks remain.              |
|                                                                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
